    "experiments start": "Start the draft as a new Run; ends any running Run (Policy-gated). Freezes draft Targeting Rules into the Run.

Usage:
  splitch experiments start <experiment-id> [flags]

Flags:
  --app <app>              [type: string; default: SPLITCH_APP or config] App ID or slug.
  --env <environment>      [type: string; default: SPLITCH_ENV or config] Environment ID or key.
  --by <mode>              [type: string; default: automatic] Allowed: id. Resolve a canonical-looking selector explicitly.
  --body-json <json>       [type: JSON object; default: none] Request body fields; see Request body below.
  --idempotency-key <key>  [type: string; default: generated] Stable retry key for this mutation.
  --confirm                [type: boolean; default: false] Approve and apply a Policy-gated change.
  --json                   [type: boolean; default: false] Write machine-readable JSON to stdout.
  -h, --help               [type: boolean; default: false] Show help and exit.

Request body (--body-json):
  review            optional  { action: "approve_and_apply" }
  reason            optional  string
  horizon           optional  string ("sequential" | "fixed")
  sampleSizeLocked  optional  number | null
  idempotency_key   required  string
  Example:
    {"horizon":"sequential","idempotency_key":"idem-1"}

Behavior:
  Start freezes the Experiment draft Targeting Rules into the new Run; the response field frozenTargetingRules is that snapshot (same as run.targetingRules).
  An empty frozen set means all Entities are eligible via allocation; Flag Configuration targeting rules do not apply while the Run is live.
  Without --json, the CLI prints a frozen-targeting summary to stderr.

Example:
  splitch experiments start <experiment-id> --body-json '{"horizon":"sequential","idempotency_key":"idem-1"}' --json",
    "experiments update": "Edit an Experiment (assignment edits gated by RUN_FROZEN / DECISION_LOCKED). Staged Targeting Rule edits under a live Run leave evaluation on the frozen Run.

Usage:
  splitch experiments update <experiment-id> [flags]

Flags:
  --app <app>          [type: string; default: SPLITCH_APP or config] App ID or slug.
  --env <environment>  [type: string; default: SPLITCH_ENV or config] Environment ID or key.
  --name <name>        [type: string; default: none] Resource name.
  --by <mode>          [type: string; default: automatic] Allowed: id. Resolve a canonical-looking selector explicitly.
  --body-json <json>   [type: JSON object; default: none] Request body fields; see Request body below.
  --json               [type: boolean; default: false] Write machine-readable JSON to stdout.
  -h, --help           [type: boolean; default: false] Show help and exit.

Request body (--body-json):
  name                optional  string
  description         optional  string
  hypothesis          optional  string
  owner               optional  string
  tags                optional  string[]
  flagId              optional  string
  targetingKey        optional  string
  targetingKeyType    optional  string
  activationMetricId  optional  string | null
  allocation          optional  Record<Variant name, number>
  salt                optional  string
  variantSet          optional  { id: string; name: string; value: boolean | string | number | Record<string, unknown>; description?: string }[]
  targetingRules      optional  { id: string; flagId: string; priority: number; conditions: { attribute: string; operator: string ("eq" | "neq" | "gt" | "lt" | "gte" | "lte" | "in" | "not_in" | "matches" | "not_matches"); value: boolean | string | number | (boolean | string | number)[] }[]; variantId: string; segmentId?: string; percentageRollout?: { percentage: number; salt?: string } | null }[]
  segmentIds          optional  string[]
  metrics             optional  { metricId: string }[]
  guardrailMetrics    optional  { metricId: string }[]
  conversionWindowMs  optional  number
  dimensions          optional  string[]
  confidenceLevel     optional  number
  stageForNextRun     optional  true
  Example:
    {"tags":[],"allocation":{"control":1,"treatment":1},"segmentIds":[],"dimensions":[],"stageForNextRun":true}

Behavior:
  While a Run is live, assignment fields (including Targeting Rules) require stageForNextRun and write only the next-Run draft.
  A successful staged Targeting Rule edit returns liveRunUnaffected naming the Run evaluation still uses.
  Without --json, the CLI prints that the live Run is unaffected.

Example:
  splitch experiments update <experiment-id> --body-json '{"tags":[],"allocation":{"control":1,"treatment":1},"segmentIds":[],"dimensions":[],"stageForNextRun":true}' --json",
