    "flag-targeting-rules add": "Append one equality Targeting Rule: serve a Variant when attributes match.

Usage:
  splitch flag-targeting-rules add <flag-id-or-key> [flags]

Flags:
  --app <app>              [type: string; default: SPLITCH_APP or config] App ID or slug.
  --env <environment>      [type: string; default: SPLITCH_ENV or config] Environment ID or key.
  --by <mode>              [type: string; default: automatic] Allowed: id. Resolve a canonical-looking selector explicitly.
  --when <attr=value>      [type: string (repeatable); default: none] String equality Condition. Repeat to AND more. Number or boolean values require replace --body-json.
  --serve <variant>        [type: string; default: none] Catalog Variant name to serve when the Conditions match.
  --idempotency-key <key>  [type: string; default: generated] Stable retry key for this mutation.
  --confirm                [type: boolean; default: false] Approve and apply a Policy-gated change.
  --json                   [type: boolean; default: false] Write machine-readable JSON to stdout.
  -h, --help               [type: boolean; default: false] Show help and exit.

Behavior:
  Builds one equality Targeting Rule (id, Variant name, schema) and appends it via flag-targeting-rules replace.
  Repeat --when to AND Conditions. Each value is a string (evaluation uses Object.is); shell quoting cannot change the type. Number or boolean Conditions require flag-targeting-rules replace --body-json.
  For Segments, non-equality operators, OR groups, reordering, or removal, use flag-targeting-rules replace.
  Read-modify-write is last-write-wins: a concurrent replace can overwrite this append. The replace endpoint has no version guard.
  While a Run is live on this Flag, the write is refused with RUN_FROZEN (end the Run first).

Example:
  splitch flag-targeting-rules add checkout --when plan=enterprise --serve on --json",
    "flag-targeting-rules replace": "Full-replace this Environment's ordered Targeting Rule list.

Usage:
  splitch flag-targeting-rules replace <flag-id-or-key> [flags]

Flags:
  --app <app>              [type: string; default: SPLITCH_APP or config] App ID or slug.
  --env <environment>      [type: string; default: SPLITCH_ENV or config] Environment ID or key.
  --by <mode>              [type: string; default: automatic] Allowed: id. Resolve a canonical-looking selector explicitly.
  --body-json <json>       [type: JSON object; default: none] Request body fields; see Request body below.
  --idempotency-key <key>  [type: string; default: generated] Stable retry key for this mutation.
  --confirm                [type: boolean; default: false] Approve and apply a Policy-gated change.
  --json                   [type: boolean; default: false] Write machine-readable JSON to stdout.
  -h, --help               [type: boolean; default: false] Show help and exit.

Request body (--body-json):
  targetingRules   required  { id: string; flagId: string; priority: number; conditions: { attribute: string; operator: string ("eq" | "neq" | "gt" | "lt" | "gte" | "lte" | "in" | "not_in" | "matches" | "not_matches"); value: boolean | string | number | (boolean | string | number)[] }[]; variantId: string; segmentId?: string; percentageRollout?: { percentage: number; salt?: string } | null }[]
  review           optional  { action: "approve_and_apply" }
  idempotency_key  required  string
  Example:
    {"targetingRules":[{"id":"id_1","flagId":"flag_1","priority":0,"conditions":[{"attribute":"attribute","operator":"eq","value":true}],"variantId":"variant_1"}],"idempotency_key":"idem-1"}

Behavior:
  Raw full-replace of this Environment's ordered Targeting Rule list. For the common equality case, use flag-targeting-rules add --when attr=value --serve <variant>.
  Read-modify-write is last-write-wins: send the complete list, or a concurrent writer drops rules you omitted. The replace endpoint has no version guard.
  While a Run is live on this Flag, replace is refused with RUN_FROZEN (end the Run first).
  A live Run evaluates its own frozen Targeting Rule snapshot, not this Flag Configuration list.

Example:
  splitch flag-targeting-rules replace <flag-id-or-key> --body-json '{"targetingRules":[{"id":"id_1","flagId":"flag_1","priority":0,"conditions":[{"attribute":"attribute","operator":"eq","value":true}],"variantId":"variant_1"}],"idempotency_key":"idem-1"}' --json",
